abus.","topic":"2.3","objectives":[],"metadata":{"source":"structured-cambridge-dataset","section":"2"}},{"level":"as_level","kind":"learning_outcome","knowledge_key":"outcome|2.3|1","title":"Formulas: outcome 1","content":"Write ionic-compound formulas from ionic charges and oxidation numbers, including predicting ionic charge from Periodic Table position and recalling the specified common ions.","topic":"2.3","objectives":["2.3.1"],"metadata":{"source":"structured-cambridge-dataset","section":"2","outcomeIndex":1}},{"level":"as_level","kind":"learning_outcome","knowledge_key":"outcome|2.3|2","title":"Formulas: outcome 2","content":"Write and construct balanced equations, including ionic equations without spectator ions, and use appropriate state symbols.","topic":"2.3","objectives":["2.3.2"],"metadata":{"source":"structured-cambridge-dataset","section":"2","outcomeIndex":2}},{"level":"as_level","kind":"learning_outcome","knowledge_key":"outcome|2.3|3","title":"Formulas: outcome 3","content":"Define and use empirical and molecular formula.","topic":"2.3","objectives":["2.3.3"],"metadata":{"source":"structured-cambridge-dataset","section":"2","outcomeIndex":3}},{"level":"as_level","kind":"learning_outcome","knowledge_key":"outcome|2.3|4","title":"Formulas: outcome 4","content":"Understand and use anhydrous, hydrated and water of crystallisation.","topic":"2.3","objectives":["2.3.4"],"metadata":{"source":"structured-cambridge-dataset","section":"2","outcomeIndex":4}},{"level":"as_level","kind":"learning_outcome","knowledge_key":"outcome|2.3|5","title":"Formulas: outcome 5","content":"Calculate empirical and molecular formulas from given data.","topic":"2.3","objectives":["2.3.5"],"metadata":{"source":"structured-cambridge-dataset","section":"2","outcomeIndex":5}},{"level":"as_level","kind":"topic","knowledge_key":"topic|2.4","title":"Reacting masses and volumes (of solutions and gases)","content":"2.4 Reacting masses and volumes (of solutions and gases)","topic":"2.4","objectives":[],"metadata":{"source":"structured-cambridge-dataset","section":"2"}},{"level":"as_level","kind":"content_scope","knowledge_key":"scope|2.4","title":"Reacting masses and volumes (of solutions and gases)","content":"Atoms, molecules and stoichiometry: Reacting masses and volumes (of solutions and gases). This subsection is part of the AS Level Chemistry 9701 syllabus.","topic":"2.4","objectives":[],"metadata":{"source":"structured-cambridge-dataset","section":"2"}},{"level":"as_level","kind":"learning_outcome","knowledge_key":"outcome|2.4|1","title":"Reacting masses and volumes (of solutions and gases): outcome 1","content":"Perform mole calculations involving reacting masses and percentage yield, gas volumes, solution volumes and concentrations, and limiting and excess reagents.","topic":"2.4","objectives":["2.4.1"],"metadata":{"source":"structured-cambridge-dataset","section":"2","outcomeIndex":1}},{"level":"as_level","kind":"learning_outcome","knowledge_key":"outcome|2.4|2","title":"Reacting masses and volumes (of solutions and gases): outcome 2","content":"Deduce stoichiometric relationships from mole calculations.","topic":"2.4","objectives":["2.4.2"],"metadata":{"source":"structured-cambridge-dataset","section":"2","outcomeIndex":2}},{"level":"as_level","kind":"topic","knowledge_key":"topic|3","title":"Chemical bonding","content":"Topic 3: Chemical bonding.","topic":"3","objectives":[],"metadata":{"source":"structured-cambridge-dataset","section":"3"}},{"level":"as_level","kind":"topic","knowledge_key":"topic|3.1","title":"Electronegativity and bonding","content":"3.1 Electronegativity and bonding","topic":"3.1","objectives":[],"metadata":{"source":"structured-cambridge-dataset","section":"3"}},{"level":"as_level","kind":"content_scope","knowledge_key":"scope|3.1","title":"Electronegativity and bonding","content":"Chemical bonding: Electronegativity and bonding. This subsection is part of the AS Level Chemistry 9701 syllabus.","topic":"3.1","objectives":[],"metadata":{"source":"structured-cambridge-dataset","section":"3"}},{"level":"as_level","kind":"learning_outcome","knowledge_key":"outcome|3.1|1","title":"Electronegativity and bonding: outcome 1","content":"Define electronegativity as the power of an atom to attract electrons to itself.","topic":"3.1","objectives":["3.1.1"],"metadata":{"source":"structured-cambridge-dataset","section":"3","outcomeIndex":1}},{"level":"as_level","kind":"learning_outcome","knowledge_key":"outcome|3.1|2","title":"Electronegativity and bonding: outcome 2","content":"Explain factors influencing electronegativity using nuclear charge, atomic radius and shielding by inner shells and sub-shells.","topic":"3.1","objectives":["3.1.2"],"metadata":{"source":"structured-cambridge-dataset","section":"3","outcomeIndex":2}},{"level":"as_level","kind":"learning_outcome","knowledge_key":"outcome|3.1|3","title":"Electronegativity and bonding: outcome 3","content":"State and explain electronegativity trends across a period and down a group.","topic":"3.1","objectives":["3.1.3"],"metadata":{"source":"structured-cambridge-dataset","section":"3","outcomeIndex":3}},{"level":"as_level","kind":"learning_outcome","knowledge_key":"outcome|3.1|4","title":"Electronegativity and bonding: outcome 4","content":"Use Pauling electronegativity differences to predict ionic or covalent bonding.","topic":"3.1","objectives":["3.1.4"],"metadata":{"source":"structured-cambridge-dataset","section":"3","outcomeIndex":4}},{"level":"as_level","kind":"topic","knowledge_key":"topic|3.2","title":"Ionic bonding","content":"3.2 Ionic bonding","topic":"3.2","objectives":[],"metadata":{"source":"structured-cambridge-dataset","section":"3"}},{"level":"as_level","kind":"content_scope","knowledge_key":"scope|3.2","title":"Ionic bonding","content":"Chemical bonding: Ionic bonding. This subsection is part of the AS Level Chemistry 9701 syllabus.","topic":"3.2","objectives":[],"metadata":{"source":"structured-cambridge-dataset","section":"3"}},{"level":"as_level","kind":"learning_outcome","knowledge_key":"outcome|3.2|1","title":"Ionic bonding: outcome 1","content":"Define ionic bonding as electrostatic attraction between oppositely charged ions.","topic":"3.2","objectives":["3.2.1"],"metadata":{"source":"structured-cambridge-dataset","section":"3","outcomeIndex":1}},{"level":"as_level","kind":"learning_outcome","knowledge_key":"outcome|3.2|2","title":"Ionic bonding: outcome 2","content":"Describe ionic bonding using sodium chloride, magnesium oxide and calcium fluoride as examples.","topic":"3.2","objectives":["3.2.2"],"metadata":{"source":"structured-cambridge-dataset","section":"3","outcomeIndex":2}},{"level":"as_level","kind":"topic","knowledge_key":"topic|3.3","title":"Metallic bonding","content":"3.3 Metallic bonding","topic":"3.3","objectives":[],"metadata":{"source":"structured-cambridge-dataset","section":"3"}},{"level":"as_level","kind":"content_scope","knowledge_key":"scope|3.3","title":"Metallic bonding","content":"Chemical bonding: Metallic bonding. This subsection is part of the AS Level Chemistry 9701 syllabus.","topic":"3.3","objectives":[],"metadata":{"source":"structured-cambridge-dataset","section":"3"}},{"level":"as_level","kind":"learning_outcome","knowledge_key":"outcome|3.3|1","title":"Metallic bonding: outcome 1","content":"Define metallic bonding as electrostatic attraction between positive metal ions and delocalised electrons.","topic":"3.3","objectives":["3.3.1"],"metadata":{"source":"structured-cambridge-dataset","section":"3","outcomeIndex":1}},{"level":"as_level","kind":"topic","knowledge_key":"topic|3.4","title":"Covalent bonding and coordinate (dative covalent) bonding","content":"3.4 Covalent bonding and coordinate (dative covalent) bonding","topic":"3.4","objectives":[],"metadata":{"source":"structured-cambridge-dataset","section":"3"}},{"level":"as_level","kind":"content_scope","knowledge_key":"scope|3.4","title":"Covalent bonding and coordinate (dative covalent) bonding","content":"Chemical bonding: Covalent bonding and coordinate (dative covalent) bonding. This subsection is part of the AS Level Chemistry 9701 syllabus.","topic":"3.4","objectives":[],"metadata":{"source":"structured-cambridge-dataset","section":"3"}},{"level":"as_level","kind":"learning_outcome","knowledge_key":"outcome|3.4|1","title":"Covalent bonding and coordinate (dative covalent) bonding: outcome 1","content":"Define covalent bonding as electrostatic attraction between two nuclei and a shared pair of electrons, and describe covalent bonding in the specified molecules and expanded-octet examples.","topic":"3.4","objectives":["3.4.1"],"metadata":{"source":"structured-cambridge-dataset","section":"3","outcomeIndex":1}},{"level":"as_level","kind":"learning_outcome","knowledge_key":"outcome|3.4|2","title":"Covalent bonding and coordinate (dative covalent) bonding: outcome 2","content":"Describe coordinate (dative covalent) bonding, including formation of NH4+ from ammonia and hydrogen chloride and bonding in Al2Cl6.","topic":"3.4","objectives":["3.4.2"],"metadata":{"source":"structured-cambridge-dataset","section":"3","outcomeIndex":2}},{"level":"as_level","kind":"learning_outcome","knowledge_key":"outcome|3.4|3","title":"Covalent bonding and coordinate (dative covalent) bonding: outcome 3","content":"Describe sigma and pi bonds using orbital overlap, apply them to H2, C2H6, C2H4, HCN and N2, use sp/sp2/sp3 hybridisation, and relate bond energy and bond length to covalent reactivity.","topic":"3.4","objectives":["3.4.3"],"metadata":{"source":"structured-cambridge-dataset","section":"3","outcomeIndex":3}},{"level":"as_level","kind":"topic","knowledge_key":"topic|3.5","title":"Shapes of molecules","content":"3.5 Shapes of molecules","topic":"3.5","objectives":[],"metadata":{"source":"structured-cambridge-dataset","section":"3"}},{"level":"as_level","kind":"content_scope","knowledge_key":"scope|3.5","title":"Shapes of molecules","content":"Chemical bonding: Shapes of molecules. This subsection is part of the AS Level Chemistry 9701 syllabus.","topic":"3.5","objectives":[],"metadata":{"source":"structured-cambridge-dataset","section":"3"}},{"level":"as_level","kind":"learning_outcome","knowledge_key":"outcome|3.5|1","title":"Shapes of molecules: outcome 1","content":"State and explain molecular shapes and bond angles using VSEPR theory for the specified examples.","topic":"3.5","objectives":["3.5.1"],"metadata":{"source":"structured-cambridge-dataset","section":"3","outcomeIndex":1}},{"level":"as_level","kind":"learning_outcome","knowledge_key":"outcome|3.5|2","title":"Shapes of molecules: outcome 2","content":"Predict shapes and bond angles in molecules and ions analogous to the specified VSEPR examples.","topic":"3.5","objectives":["3.5.2"],"metadata":{"source":"structured-cambridge-dataset","section":"3","outcomeIndex":2}},{"level":"as_level","kind":"topic","knowledge_key":"topic|3.6","title":"Intermolecular forces, electronegativity and bond properties","content":"3.6 Intermolecular forces, electronegativity and bond properties","topic":"3.6","objectives":[],"metadata":{"source":"structured-cambridge-dataset","section":"3"}},{"level":"as_level","kind":"content_scope","knowledge_key":"scope|3.6","title":"Intermolecular forces, electronegativity and bond properties","content":"Chemical bonding: Intermolecular forces, electronegativity and bond properties. This subsection is part of the AS Level Chemistry 9701 syllabus.","topic":"3.6","objectives":[],"metadata":{"source":"structured-cambridge-dataset","section":"3"}},{"level":"as_level","kind":"learning_outcome","knowledge_key":"outcome|3.6|1","title":"Intermolecular forces, electronegativity and bond properties: outcome 1","content":"Describe hydrogen bonding in molecules containing N-H and O-H groups and use it to explain anomalous properties of water.","topic":"3.6","objectives":["3.6.1"],"metadata":{"source":"structured-cambridge-dataset","section":"3","outc