ndicator or conductivity.","topic":"7.2","objectives":["7.2.6"],"metadata":{"source":"structured-cambridge-dataset","section":"7","outcomeIndex":6}},{"level":"as_level","kind":"learning_outcome","knowledge_key":"outcome|7.2|7","title":"Brønsted–Lowry theory of acids and bases: outcome 7","content":"Understand neutralisation as H+(aq) and OH-(aq) forming H2O(l).","topic":"7.2","objectives":["7.2.7"],"metadata":{"source":"structured-cambridge-dataset","section":"7","outcomeIndex":7}},{"level":"as_level","kind":"learning_outcome","knowledge_key":"outcome|7.2|8","title":"Brønsted–Lowry theory of acids and bases: outcome 8","content":"Understand that salts are formed in neutralisation reactions.","topic":"7.2","objectives":["7.2.8"],"metadata":{"source":"structured-cambridge-dataset","section":"7","outcomeIndex":8}},{"level":"as_level","kind":"learning_outcome","knowledge_key":"outcome|7.2|9","title":"Brønsted–Lowry theory of acids and bases: outcome 9","content":"Sketch pH titration curves for combinations of strong and weak acids with strong and weak alkalis.","topic":"7.2","objectives":["7.2.9"],"metadata":{"source":"structured-cambridge-dataset","section":"7","outcomeIndex":9}},{"level":"as_level","kind":"learning_outcome","knowledge_key":"outcome|7.2|10","title":"Brønsted–Lowry theory of acids and bases: outcome 10","content":"Select suitable indicators for acid-alkali titrations from given data.","topic":"7.2","objectives":["7.2.10"],"metadata":{"source":"structured-cambridge-dataset","section":"7","outcomeIndex":10}},{"level":"as_level","kind":"topic","knowledge_key":"topic|8","title":"Reaction kinetics","content":"Topic 8: Reaction kinetics.","topic":"8","objectives":[],"metadata":{"source":"structured-cambridge-dataset","section":"8"}},{"level":"as_level","kind":"topic","knowledge_key":"topic|8.1","title":"Rate of reaction","content":"8.1 Rate of reaction","topic":"8.1","objectives":[],"metadata":{"source":"structured-cambridge-dataset","section":"8"}},{"level":